# A small JEPA world model on a low-cost arm: the encoder reads the outcome, the predictor adds nothing to it

*Ravindu Somawansa, Shisso Robotics, Paris — draft. Every number below is generated by `paper/make_paper.py` from `repro/*/expected/`; its source is in `paper/numbers.json`.*

## Abstract

A small JEPA world model trained on a low-cost arm reads how an episode ended, and its predictor adds nothing to that reading. We trained the model in the LeWorldModel recipe on teleoperated demonstrations of one tabletop task on an SO-101 arm, then re-tested on it every use of such a model we found in recent papers, each against a bar written into a public register. Every use that needs the predictor to roll forward misses its bar, chunk ranking, surprise monitoring and risk prediction among them. The encoder does better. On policy rollouts it never saw, a linear probe on its last-frame latent separates success from failure about as well as a hand-built colour judge (AUROC {{rest_W1_2}} against {{rest_judge_2}}), and better than a frozen DINOv2 encoder or the commanded joints. At the grasp, the imagined latent reads the outcome worse than the current one ({{c1_imagined}} against {{c1_current}}). So before building on the predictor of a model this size, probe its encoder. The controls are exploratory, and everything comes from one rig, one model and one data scale.

## Introduction

A world model is usually valued for its predictor. In the sense of LeWorldModel (LeWM) [@maes2026lewm] it has two parts: an encoder that maps camera frames to a latent vector, and a predictor that takes the current latent and a sequence of actions and imagines the latents that follow, both trained together from pixels with a joint-embedding predictive (JEPA) loss. The uses proposed for it lean on the predictor: ranking a policy's candidate action chunks by their imagined outcome, flagging a failure when the observed latent drifts from the predicted one, reading the outcome of a grasp before it happens, planning over several time scales. Each paper reports the use that worked, on its own robot and data.

We had one such model, trained for ${{cost_w1}} of rented GPU time on our own arm, and wanted to know which of these uses we could build on. So we ran every use we could find on that one model. Each re-test is a row of a register with a bar, a dataset, a result and a verdict, and where we managed it, the bar and the decision rule went into the repository before the result existed (that commit order is all we mean by pre-registration). Checked against the commit history, all {{prereg_matched}} rows match a pre-registration record. In {{bar_first}} the bar was committed before the result, and in {{same_commit}} (the two-level hierarchical progress use) in the same commit. One row is not a use of our model: it times the pretrained RoboJEPA and V-JEPA 2.1 encoders on our on-board computer [@zholus2026robojepa; @murlabadia2026vjepa21]. Of the {{rows}} rows, {{n_pass}} pass and {{n_met}} is met as predicted. Another {{n_np}} are not proven, and the remaining {{n_fail}} fail or are no-go.

The first draft of this paper argued something else. Our reading of the failures was that the latent mostly encodes the arm's pose, so a frozen image encoder or the commanded joints would do at least as well in every one of them. We ran those controls on {{n}} policy rollouts before writing it up, and they did not bear it out: the encoder reads how the episode went better than either control. We rewrote the claim. At this data scale the predictor, not the encoder, is the weak link.

One moment runs through the rest of the paper: the grasp anchor, {{anc_lead}} frames before the gripper first closes. There the current latent reads the outcome at {{c1_current}} and the imagined latent at {{c1_imagined}}, and that gap is the argument in one row.

The controls came after the data, which limits what they can show. Each readout is a linear probe fit with leave-one-session-out (LOSO) cross-validation on the {{n}} rollouts ({{success}} success, {{failure}} failure, {{sessions}} sessions), scored against labels the author gave at rest. The model was trained on teleoperation only and never saw these rollouts. No bar was committed for these comparisons, so everything this paper says about the split between encoder and predictor is exploratory. Of the {{n_pass}} register passes, {{pass_bar_first}} have their bar committed before the result, though the register marks the outcome-readout verdict as retrospective.

## Setup

The robot is one SO-101 arm [@so101] driven through LeRobot [@cadene2024lerobot], with a top and a wrist camera. The task is to put a bowl into another bowl. Our model is trained on `ravediamond/so101_bowls_all_trimmed`, split by grid layout into {{train_eps}} training and {{val_eps}} validation episodes. The evaluation rollouts come from a separate dataset, `ravediamond/so101-bowls-policy-rollouts`: SmolVLA [@shukor2025smolvla] runs recorded after our model was trained.

Our model follows the LeWM recipe [@maes2026lewm], with a multi-view ViT encoder, an action-conditioned predictor and the SIGReg regulariser. We set it beside four signals, each chosen because it is cheaper than a world model. The frozen DINOv2-S control is a frozen DINOv2 small encoder [@oquab2023dinov2] mean-pooled over the top and wrist views (the design of DINO-WM [@zhou2024dinowm]). The joints control is the commanded joint vector. The colour judge is a hand-built rule on the final frame with no learning, and policy identity is the checkpoint that ran the episode.

In October, a timing test on the on-board computer loaded our checkpoint with `strict=False`. Under the transformers version on that image ({{audit_tf}}), {{audit_dropped}} encoder tensors did not match and were silently dropped, so the test timed a random encoder. We voided its latents and traced how every other use loaded the model: {{audit_trained}} ran the trained model, {{audit_random}} ran a random one (that test) and none is unknown. The world-model block of VLA-JEPA, which one diagnostic below uses, loads all {{wm_keys}} of its keys.

## The register

Table @tab:register lists the {{rows}} uses and what each takes from our model: the encoder alone ({{uses_encoder}}), the predictor ({{uses_predictor}}) or both ({{uses_both}}). Read that column against the verdicts.

<!-- register -->

Among the encoder uses, reading the outcome works and reading progress does not. The outcome readout at rest passes, and at the grasp anchor the current latent reads the grasp at {{c1_current}}. The four uses that ask the latent for progress or task stage all fail: progress monotonicity, two-level hierarchical progress [@zhang2026hjepa], the latent stage tracker and the latent-distance scale of SURVEYOR [@surveyor2026]. The pose probes of LeJEPA [@klindt2026lejepa] say what else the latent holds. A linear readout recovers about half of the joint variance from our encoder (R² {{lejepa_w1}}), less than from the frozen DINOv2-S control ({{lejepa_w2p}}).

The predictor does predict its own latent: at K = 30 its open-loop error is {{g0_err}}, against {{g0_copy}} for copying the last latent. What fails is every use of that prediction. Chunk ranking [@maes2026lewm] puts the executed chunk first in {{g1b_top1}} of anchors against a bar of {{g1b_bar}}, while the commanded actions alone reach {{g1b_actions}} (so the cheapest signal in the table beats the use built on imagination). The surprise monitor catches {{w1a_hits}} failures. The hierarchical planner [@zhang2026hwm] shows no crossover. The conformal surprise band, the re-ranking of chunk candidates, the action-conditioned risk head [@liu2026reap] and the calibrated surprise reflex each miss their bar by the margin in Table @tab:register.

## What the encoder knows

Does the encoder carry anything that a frozen encoder or the joints do not? Table @tab:controls sets our model beside the four other signals on the same {{n}} rollouts. Every probe is a LOSO linear probe against the author's labels. At rest the interval is a 95 % episode-bootstrap CI; at the anchor it is a bootstrap of the mean over 5 seeds of the out-of-fold AUROC (episodes resampled jointly across the seeds, 2,000 resamples, percentile).

Table @tab:controls: Outcome AUROC of each signal on the same {{n}} rollouts (exploratory: controls chosen after the data). Look at the anchor column, where no judge applies.

| feature | at rest | grasp anchor ({{anc_scored}} scored, {{anc_fail}} fail) |
|---|---|---|
| our world model | {{rest_W1}} [{{rest_W1_lo}}, {{rest_W1_hi}}] | {{anc_W1}} [{{anc_W1_lo}}, {{anc_W1_hi}}] |
| colour judge (no learning) | {{rest_judge}} [{{rest_judge_lo}}, {{rest_judge_hi}}] | — |
| frozen DINOv2-S | {{rest_W2p}} [{{rest_W2p_lo}}, {{rest_W2p_hi}}] | {{anc_W2p}} [{{anc_W2p_lo}}, {{anc_W2p_hi}}] |
| commanded joints | {{rest_joints}} [{{rest_joints_lo}}, {{rest_joints_hi}}] | {{anc_joints}} [{{anc_joints_lo}}, {{anc_joints_hi}}] |
| policy identity | {{rest_policy}} [{{rest_policy_lo}}, {{rest_policy_hi}}] | — |

![Table @tab:controls as a dot plot: level with the colour judge at rest, ahead of both controls at the anchor. The right panel shows the seed range, not a CI.](fig/table2.pdf){#fig:controls}

At rest our model is level with the colour judge (the CIs overlap) and above the frozen encoder and the joints. At the grasp anchor no judge applies, and our model leads both controls. Since the pose probes hold the joints better than our encoder does but the outcome worse, the encoder carries outcome information they lack, at least on these rollouts.

The anchor column depends on how the five seeds are combined. Averaging the predictions before scoring gives {{anc_W1_avg}}, {{anc_W2p_avg}} and {{anc_joints_avg}} for our model, frozen DINOv2-S and the joints. Seed 0 alone gives {{anc_W1_s0}}, {{anc_W2p_s0}} and {{anc_joints_s0}}, a smaller gap between our model and the frozen encoder. The order holds either way.

One control does beat our model. On SURVEYOR's scale check, frozen DINOv2-S (τ {{surv_tau_W2p}}) and the joints (τ {{surv_tau_joints}}) pass where our model fails (τ {{surv_tau_W1}}, against {{surv_paper}} reported by SURVEYOR). The same task and the same rollouts pass with other features, so the failure points at the geometry of our latent space rather than at the task.

The register's own outcome-readout row reports {{g10_rest}}. That number was scored against the colour judge's labels, on {{g10_n}} rollouts, so it partly measures agreement with the judge. Against the author's labels on the {{n}} rollouts, with the controls beside it, the same readout gives {{rest_W1}}.

## Where the predictor fails

ContactGuard [@zheng2026contactguard] finds that probes on the imagined latent beat probes on the current one for grasp tasks, so we expected the imagined latent to help at our grasp anchor. The bar asked it for {{c1_bar}} over the current latent. With the same encoder and the same anchor, the current latent reads the outcome at {{c1_current}} and the imagined one at {{c1_imagined}}. This pre-registered row fails: on these rollouts, imagining forward loses outcome information the encoder had.

Our model's inverse-dynamics term is no better than that of its own untrained network. On the distillation probe, pooled over the {{g5_val}} teleoperation validation episodes, the trained model scores {{g5_w1}} and its random initialisation {{g5_rand}}, a difference of {{g5_diff}} with a 95 % paired episode-bootstrap CI of [{{g5_lo}}, {{g5_hi}}] (`gap5_pooled_rerun.json`). The term itself is fragile: the same untrained network scored {{g5_unstable}} on one machine and {{g5_rand}} and {{g5_orin}} on two others (it is sensitive to the BLAS library and to ridge conditioning).

A low score can also come from a bug, so we checked the surprise monitor's failure-class AUROC ({{m1_auroc}}, below its sanity bar) for one in loading or scoring. The same scorer reproduces the open-loop corruption ratios at K = 30: cross-episode {{m1_xep}} against {{m1_xep_g0}} in the original run, constant-first {{m1_cf}} against {{m1_cf_g0}} (`M1_diag_g0_repro.json`). The low AUROC is a real value.

The predictor of VLA-JEPA barely uses its actions. At {{m3_n}} anchors (Table @tab:vlajepa, from `M3_diag_action_shuffle.json`), action tokens taken from another episode differ from the true ones by {{m3_tok_lo}}–{{m3_tok_hi}} % in relative L2 but move the loss by only about {{m3_sh}} %. Zeroing the tokens moves it by about {{m3_zero}} % on average, and the forward pass is deterministic. Its failure-class AUROC of {{m3_auroc}}, below the same sanity bar, is therefore real, and we leave its table unread under the frozen bar.

Table @tab:vlajepa: VLA-JEPA world-model loss at {{m3_n}} anchors with true, shuffled (Δ shuffled) and zeroed action tokens (exploratory spot check). The loss barely moves although the shuffled tokens differ by {{m3_tok_lo}} % or more.

| anchor (episode / frame) | true loss | Δ shuffled (%) | token change (%) | Δ zeroed (%) |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| {{m3_0_ep}} / {{m3_0_fr}} | {{m3_0_true}} | {{m3_0_sh}} | {{m3_0_tok}} | {{m3_0_zero}} |
| {{m3_1_ep}} / {{m3_1_fr}} | {{m3_1_true}} | {{m3_1_sh}} | {{m3_1_tok}} | {{m3_1_zero}} |
| {{m3_2_ep}} / {{m3_2_fr}} | {{m3_2_true}} | {{m3_2_sh}} | {{m3_2_tok}} | {{m3_2_zero}} |
| {{m3_3_ep}} / {{m3_3_fr}} | {{m3_3_true}} | {{m3_3_sh}} | {{m3_3_tok}} | {{m3_3_zero}} |
| {{m3_4_ep}} / {{m3_4_fr}} | {{m3_4_true}} | {{m3_4_sh}} | {{m3_4_tok}} | {{m3_4_zero}} |

A reader may object that one pass in the register is predictive. In the anticipation step of the outcome-readout gate, the imagined latent beat policy identity by {{g10_anticip}}. That bar is retrospective, though, and the two reruns of the same idea with the bar committed first, the grasp probe and the calibrated surprise reflex, both fail.

## Related work

LeWorldModel [@maes2026lewm] trains the recipe we use end to end from pixels and plans with it in Push-T and in simulation. Its two planning uses, chunk ranking and re-ranking, fail on our arm. LeJEPA's identifiability analysis [@klindt2026lejepa] predicts that SIGReg latents identify the state up to anisotropy; our pose probes support that only in part. DINO-WM [@zhou2024dinowm] plans on frozen DINOv2 [@oquab2023dinov2] patches, which is why our frozen control is built that way. V-JEPA 2.1 [@murlabadia2026vjepa21] and RoboJEPA [@zholus2026robojepa] are too slow on our on-board computer; timing them is the one register row that is not a use of our model. ContactGuard [@zheng2026contactguard] is the result we contradict most directly: it finds imagined-latent probes better than current-latent ones on grasp tasks, and we find the reverse. The re-tests of ReAP's action-conditioned risk [@liu2026reap], the hierarchical planners of HWM [@zhang2026hwm] and H-JEPA [@zhang2026hjepa] and SURVEYOR's latent-distance scale [@surveyor2026] all miss their bars here. Two papers agree with us from the control side. SaiVLA-JEPA [@xiao2026saivla] reports that a training-only future-latent head adds {{sai_gain}} points to control on {{sai_eps}} paired episodes, and HIJP [@hijp2026] finds LeWM below goal-conditioned behaviour cloning in simulation.

## Limitations

The claim most likely to move is the split between encoder and predictor, because it rests on probes chosen after the data and on {{n}} rollouts labelled by the author. Fresh rollouts scored under a bar committed first would test it. They need a day on the arm, which is not scheduled yet. The second risk is scale: everything comes from one model at one data scale, and a second point at {{scale_eps}} episodes is costed and queued. Three controls were not run (the surprise monitor and the imagined arm of the grasp probe would each need a predictor trained on frozen DINOv2-S features or on the joints, and progress monotonicity with the controls would need a reimplementation). The outcome-readout verdict is retrospective, and the two-level hierarchical progress use has its bar in the same commit as its result. Ordered by commit alone, without the audit, the register would read {{prereg_v1_bar_first}} bar-first and {{prereg_v1_same_commit}} same-commit rows.

## Conclusion

Before building on the predictor of a world model this size, probe its encoder and keep the commanded actions as a baseline. On our {{teleop_eps}} demonstrations the current latent read the grasp outcome better than the imagined one, the commanded actions alone ranked chunks better than the predictor, and every forecasting use missed its bar. A predictor may earn its place at a larger data scale, which is the next point we will measure.
